
[[File:Entrance Cottage for sale in Ashton near Oundle - geograph.org.uk - 4396837.jpg|thumb|right|200px|When city dwellers flock to rural areas and start to buy cottages as summer retreats, this can drive up house prices in these countryside regions, creating a pecuniary externality. ]]

A '''pecuniary externality''' occurs when the actions of an [[Agent (economics)|economic agent]] cause an increase or decrease in market prices separate from the impact of those actions on the quantity of resources in the market. For example, an influx of city-dwellers buying second homes in a rural area can drive up house prices, making it difficult for young people in the area to buy a house. The [[externality]] operates through prices rather than through real resource effects. 

This is in contrast with '''technological''' or '''real [[externalities]]''' that have a direct resource effect on a third party. For example, [[pollution]] from a factory directly harms the environment.  As with real externalities, pecuniary externalities can be either positive (favorable, as when consumers face a lower price) or negative (unfavorable, as when they face a higher price). The distinction between pecuniary and technological externalities was originally introduced by [[Jacob Viner]], who did not use the term ''externalities'' explicitly but distinguished between ''economies'' (positive externalities) and ''diseconomies'' (negative externalities) in 1932.<ref>Jacob Viner (1932) "Cost Curves and Supply Curves", ''Journal of Economics'', vol. 3, pp. 23-46.</ref> [[Roland McKean]] distinguished between technological and pecuniary effects by name in 1958.<ref>{{cite book|last=McKean|first=Roland N.|date=1958|title=Efficiency in Government through Systems Analysis|location=New York|publisher=John Wiley and Sons, Inc.|series=A RAND Corporation Research Study|page=386|issn=0079-7723}}</ref>

Under [[complete market|complete markets]], pecuniary externalities offset each other. For example, if someone buys whiskey and this raises the price of whiskey, the other consumers of whiskey will be worse off and the producers of whiskey will be better off. However, the loss to consumers is precisely offset by the gain to producers; therefore the resulting equilibrium is still [[Pareto efficient]].<ref>[[Jean-Jacques Laffont]] (2008). "Externalities," ''[[The New Palgrave Dictionary of Economics]]'', 2nd Edition. [http://www.dictionaryofeconomics.com/article?id=pde2008_E000200&q=externality&topicid=&result_number=9 Abstract.]</ref> As a result, some economists have suggested that pecuniary externalities are not really externalities and should not be called such. 

However, when markets are incomplete or constrained, then pecuniary externalities are relevant for Pareto efficiency.<ref name=quarterly>{{cite journal|author1=Bruce Greenwald|author2=Joseph Stiglitz|authorlink1=Bruce Greenwald|authorlink2=Joseph Stiglitz|title=Externalities in economies with imperfect information and incomplete markets|journal=[[Quarterly Journal of Economics]]|date=May 1986|volume=101|issue=2|pages=229–264|url=http://socsci2.ucsd.edu/~aronatas/project/academic/Stiglitz%20Greenwald.pdf|doi=10.2307/1891114|jstor=1891114|doi-access=free}}</ref> The reason is that under [[incomplete markets]], the relative marginal utilities of agents are not equalized. Therefore, the welfare effects of a price movement on consumers and producers do not generally offset each other.

This inefficiency is particularly relevant in [[financial economics]]. When some agents are subject to [[credit rationing|financial constraints]], then changes in their net worth or collateral that result from pecuniary externalities may have [[Order of approximation|first order]] welfare implications. The free market equilibrium in such an environment is generally not considered Pareto efficient. This is an important welfare-theoretic justification for [[macroprudential regulation]] that may require the introduction of targeted policy tools.<ref>{{cite journal|author1=Javier Bianchi|author2=Enrique G. Mendoza|title=Overborrowing, Financial Crises and 'Macro-prudential' Taxes|journal=NBER Working Paper No. 16091|date=June 2010|doi=10.3386/w16091|url=http://graduateinstitute.ch/files/live/users/ci/dh/dg/tille/files/Class_E_527b_Doc_Sem_spring_2014/class_10_April_30/Bianchi_Mendoza_w16091.pdf|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|author1=Enrico Perotti|author1-link=Enrico Perotti|author2=Javier Suarez|title=A Pigouvian Approach to Liquidity Regulation|journal=International Journal of Central Banking|date=December 2011|url=http://dare.uva.nl/document/2/99474}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|author1=Olivier Jeanne|author2=Anton Korinek|title=Managing Credit Booms and Busts: A Pigouvian Taxation Approach|journal=NBER Working Paper No. 16377|date=September 2010|doi=10.3386/w16377|url=http://www.econ2.jhu.edu/People/Jeanne/boom-bust-2012-01-30.pdf|doi-access=free}}</ref>

== Positive and Negative Pecuniary Externalities ==
Pecuniary externalities differ from traditional [[Externality|externalities]] in their influence on the allocation of resources within markets and do not necessarily lead to inefficiencies in resource allocation in the same way as traditional externalities do since they are, by definition, pure price effects.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Worcester |first=Dean A. |date=1969 |title=Pecuniary and Technological Externality, Factor Rents, and Social Costs |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/1810682 |journal=The American Economic Review |volume=59 |issue=5 |pages=873–885 |jstor=1810682 |issn=0002-8282}}</ref> Instead, they mainly affect the distribution of wealth among market participants. Pecuniary externalities can be used to generate beneficial outcomes; however, since pecuniary externalities are [[Pareto efficiency|Pareto efficient]], they can also result in [[Monopoly|monopolies]] and other economic distortions. 

Positive pecuniary externalities occur when changes in market prices result in beneficial outcomes for participants. Pecuniary externalities can be manipulated in order to redistribute wealth in a favorable way without resulting in a [[market failure]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pecuniary Externalities in Competitive Economies with Limited Pledgeability {{!}} Richmond Fed |url=https://www.richmondfed.org/publications/research/working_papers/2019/wp_19-19 |access-date=2024-04-28 |website=www.richmondfed.org |language=en-us}}</ref> For instance, pecuniary externalities can motivate individuals or firms to adopt technologies or practices that have spillover benefits for others.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pecuniary Externalities, Segregated Exchanges, and Market Liquidity in a Diamond-Dybvig Economy with Retrade {{!}} Richmond Fed |url=https://www.richmondfed.org/publications/research/economic_quarterly/2013/q4/grochulski |access-date=2024-04-28 |website=www.richmondfed.org |language=en-us}}</ref> Subsidies or tax incentives for renewable energy appliances, such as solar panels, can boost the household demand for these appliances. The decrease in electricity costs for others due to increased use of solar panels creates positive pecuniary externalities, making it beneficial for both individual adopters and society as a whole.

Negative pecuniary externalities occur when market price adjustments result in negative consequences for participants. For example, price increases caused by market dominance or monopolistic tendencies can result in a [[Economic surplus|consumer surplus]] and disrupt the allocation of resources.<ref>Dávila, E., & Korinek, A. (2017). Pecuniary externalities in economies with financial frictions.The Review of Economic Studies, 85(1), 352–395. <nowiki>https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdx010</nowiki> </ref> Despite the potential for positive outcomes, negative pecuniary externalities can cause distortions and inefficiencies by forcing firms to exercise undue influence over markets.

